# Vendored assets (see run_policy.py's header and RUNNING.md).
#
# The visual meshes are ASCII Wavefront OBJ -- one vertex or face per line, 449,879 lines across
# the 29 files. Git treats that as text by default, so they land line-by-line in `git diff`,
# `git show`, `git log -p`, `--stat` counts, and any review of this repo. Nothing about them is
# reviewable as text and they change only by wholesale replacement, so mark them binary: diffs
# collapse to "Binary files differ" and the line counts disappear. Storage is unchanged (git
# already zlib-compresses them either way); this is purely how they are *displayed*.
#
# `binary` is git's built-in macro for `-diff -merge -text`: no textual diff, no attempted
# three-way merge, and no EOL normalization -- the last of which also keeps the bytes identical
# to their source on a CRLF checkout, which matters because these are vendored copies.
*.obj binary
*.onnx binary

# Experiment outputs (see RESULTS.md / EXPERIMENTS.md for what each run directory means).
#
# Every run under results/ drops a fixed set of artifacts: .npz/.npy trajectory dumps, .png/.gif/
# .mp4 renders, and a couple of .json metric/config sidecars. Git already treats the arrays and
# renders as binary, but the JSON is text, so a PR that adds four ablation arms shows ~44 changed
# files and several hundred lines that nobody reviews line-by-line -- they are recorded output, not
# authored code, and they drown the source diff they exist to justify.
#
# `linguist-generated=true` is GitHub's marker for exactly this: the files stay in the repo and are
# fetched on clone as before, but the PR's "Files changed" view collapses them behind a "Load diff"
# link and skips them when tallying review coverage. Nothing about storage, checkout, or local
# `git diff` changes -- this is purely how GitHub renders the review.
results/** linguist-generated=true
*.npz
